1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,911
Perfect absorption, perfect delta. The trade still lost.

2
00:00:10,361 --> 00:00:16,003
This will happen, again and again. Order flow does not guarantee wins.

3
00:00:16,453 --> 00:00:23,610
Today: risk management with order flow. So losses stay small and you stay in the game.

4
00:00:24,610 --> 00:00:29,444
Hello, this is episode twenty three of the SPIDER Order Flow series.

5
00:00:29,894 --> 00:00:35,693
Last time: the top-down method. Context, level, and confirmation.

6
00:00:36,143 --> 00:00:39,147
Today, the math of keeping that trade alive.

7
00:00:40,147 --> 00:00:47,304
First question: where does the stop go? In order flow the answer is clear: where the idea is proven wrong.

8
00:00:47,754 --> 00:00:55,879
Our absorption was at 2664. The lowest print of the absorbed zone was 2662.8.

9
00:00:56,329 --> 00:01:03,173
If price goes below that, the buyers lost. Stop a little lower, at 2662.

10
00:01:04,173 --> 00:01:10,128
A common mistake: the stop right next to entry, like 2664.5.

11
00:01:10,579 --> 00:01:15,018
That is half a point. Normal market noise alone will knock it out.

12
00:01:15,469 --> 00:01:21,033
If you want less risk, cut the size; do not put the stop inside the noise.

13
00:01:22,033 --> 00:01:30,158
Now size. Account 5,000 dollars, risk 1 percent per trade, so 50 dollars.

14
00:01:30,607 --> 00:01:38,497
Entry 2665, stop 2662, so 3 points. In gold, one lot is 100 dollars per point.

15
00:01:38,946 --> 00:01:47,461
50 divided by 300 is 0.166. Round down, 0.16 lot.

16
00:01:48,461 --> 00:01:54,889
If the stop were 6 points, the size would be half, 0.08 lot.

17
00:01:55,339 --> 00:01:59,622
The dollar risk stays 50. Only the lot changed.

18
00:02:00,073 --> 00:02:08,118
The rule: stop first, then size. Never set the stop from the size. Quant episode four says the same.

19
00:02:09,118 --> 00:02:15,388
Target the next level, 2671. Six points, so 2R.

20
00:02:15,838 --> 00:02:20,435
If the next level is less than 1R away, there is no room for the trade.

21
00:02:20,885 --> 00:02:27,155
If order flow flips on the way, like heavy selling absorption, exiting early is fine too.

22
00:02:28,155 --> 00:02:34,581
Now the most important part: when not to trade. One, around big news.

23
00:02:35,031 --> 00:02:42,841
On news, the spread can jump from 0.2 to 2 for a few seconds. Stops are useless there.

24
00:02:43,291 --> 00:02:53,819
Two, thin liquidity: the futures break, holidays. Three, after three losses in a day, stop. Done for today.

25
00:02:54,819 --> 00:02:59,102
Common mistakes. Moving the stop further away mid-trade.

26
00:02:59,553 --> 00:03:04,698
Adding lots to a losing trade. And revenge trading after a loss.

27
00:03:05,149 --> 00:03:09,433
All three undo every good order flow read.

28
00:03:10,433 --> 00:03:17,668
A quick calculation. Win rate 40 percent, each win 2R, each loss 1R.

29
00:03:18,119 --> 00:03:26,635
100 trades: 40 wins is 80R, 60 losses is 60R. Net 20R.

30
00:03:27,085 --> 00:03:34,399
So you profit even while losing most trades. But only if every loss truly stops at 1R.

31
00:03:35,399 --> 00:03:41,668
So, today in short. The stop goes where the idea is wrong, not inside the noise.

32
00:03:42,119 --> 00:03:47,918
Stop first, then size. A fixed risk, like 1 percent per trade.

33
00:03:48,368 --> 00:03:53,697
News, thin liquidity, and after three losses: no trade.

34
00:03:54,697 --> 00:04:02,664
The next episode is the last in this series: one full trade from start to finish, and a recap of the whole series.

35
00:04:03,114 --> 00:04:08,025
If this was useful, subscribe and save the playlist.

36
00:04:08,475 --> 00:04:13,543
